<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Urbino</h1><p class="st-meta">1213 CE – 1631 CE (approx.) · capital Urbino</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Montefeltro and della Rovere state in the Marche, whose ducal palace and court made Urbino a model of Renaissance civility.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-montefeltro">Montefeltro</a> family, lords of a mountainous area on the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>, received Urbino as counts in the early 13th century; the register dates the county from 1213. They ruled as papal vicars and condottieri.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federico-da-montefeltro">Federico da Montefeltro</a>, a famous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> captain, was made duke in 1474 and built the Ducal Palace and a celebrated library. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guidobaldo-da-montefeltro">Guidobaldo</a>'s court inspired Baldassare Castiglione's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-book-of-the-courtier">Book of the Courtier</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> was born in the city in 1483. In 1508 the duchy passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-i-della-rovere">Francesco Maria I della Rovere</a>, nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>, and from 1523 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-pesaro">Pesaro</a> became the main residence. The duchy was also known for maiolica.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-maria-ii-della-rovere">Francesco Maria II della Rovere</a> died without a male heir in 1631, and the duchy, already promised by agreement, devolved to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>, which ruled it until 1860.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-urbino.jpg" alt="Duchy of Urbino" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Facquis · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Duchy_of_Urbino.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1213 CE – 1631 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Urbino</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Federico da Montefeltro, Guidobaldo da Montefeltro, Francesco Maria I della Rovere</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Italian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Urbino, Urbino</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=duchy-of-urbino" aria-label="Duchy of Urbino on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4984 628 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Urbino lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5304 884L5304 942M5304 884L5310 878"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0M5310 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5304 884h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5304 884h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Urbino. <a href="/atlas?year=1422&amp;focus=duchy-of-urbino">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
